<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Trunajaya rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1674">1674</a>–<a href="/years/1680">1680</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Trunajaya war</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> rebellion was a revolt led by the Madurese prince Trunajaya against the Mataram Sultanate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Supported by Makassarese fighters, the rebels sacked the court at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a> in 1677, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> died in flight. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii">Amangkurat II</a> turned to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, whose troops crushed the rebellion by 1680 in return for large concessions.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/trunajaya-rebellion.jpg" alt="Trunajaya rebellion" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem Steelink · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Combat_In_Trunajaya_Rebellion_-_The_Crown_of_Mataram_(1890).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> took the throne of Mataram in 1646, succeeding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan-agung">Sultan Agung</a>, and his early reign was marked by executions and massacres of his political enemies. In 1659 he had his father-in-law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-pekik">Pangeran Pekik</a> and his relatives killed, which estranged his East Javanese subjects and his own son, the crown prince. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a>, a descendant of the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, had been kept at the Mataram court since Madura's annexation in 1624; after his father was executed in 1656 he married into the Kajoran family of clerics, befriended the crown prince, and in 1671 made himself master of Madura.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultanate-of-gowa">Gowa Sultanate</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/makassar-war">Makassar War</a> of 1669, bands of Makassarese soldiers turned to piracy along the coasts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. Their leader, the Kraeng of Galesong, allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> in 1675 through a marriage agreement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In September 1676 a rebel army of 9,000 crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>, and at Gegodog, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tuban">Tuban</a>, it routed a much larger Mataram force under the crown prince. Within months the rebels held nearly every coastal town between Surabaya and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a> except <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a>. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> fleet under Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-speelman">Cornelis Speelman</a> stormed Surabaya in May 1677, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> moved his capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a>, but in June 1677 other rebel forces took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a> and carried the royal treasury away.</p><p>The new king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii">Amangkurat II</a>, had neither army nor treasury. He promised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> the income of the northern harbour towns and ceded the Priangan highlands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semarang">Semarang</a>. A VOC and Mataram army took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> on 25 November 1678 in an assault led by Captain François Tack, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bugis">Bugis</a> forces under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arung-palakka">Arung Palakka</a> joined the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> surrendered to the VOC on 26 December 1679 and was stabbed by the king and his courtiers at Payak; the VOC doubted the king's claim that Trunajaya had tried to kill him but did not call him to account.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii">Amangkurat II</a> built a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">VOC</a> fort beside the royal residence, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakubuwana-i">Pangeran Puger</a>, who had held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a> as a rival king, submitted only in November 1681. The war set the precedent of the VOC backing Javanese kings in exchange for concessions, but the promised payments were not made: by 1682 the king's debt exceeded 1.5 million reals, about five times the royal treasury.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Trunajaya rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Giri joined the <b>Trunajaya rebellion</b> against Mataram and was destroyed in 1680 by Mataram and Dutch forces.</blockquote><a href="/state/giri-kedaton">Giri Kedaton</a></li></ul></section>
